data: {"level":1,"reviewed":{"level":1,"label":"concept","text":"The p-norm adds up the p-th powers of the magnitudes and takes the 1/p-th root. Two problems can\nbreak that for real inputs. The exponent must be a valid one (p at least 1, or infinity). And the\npowers themselves can overflow or underflow when the entries are extreme. Fix the second problem\nwith a scale factor, which a norm allows you to pull out exactly."},"hints_note":null}

